A C program cannot be terminated directly. However, when the program finishes executing, the computer will automatically terminate the program. There is no need to explicitly terminate a C program. The program will end when it reaches the end of the code. If you want to exit the program before it finishes executing, you can use the 'exit()' function in C, but this is not recommended as it can cause unexpected behavior in the program. Instead, it is better to use a 'return' statement to exit the program from a function. This will allow the program to properly terminate and return control to the calling function. 

For example, if you have a function that is supposed to terminate the program, you should not use 'exit()' but instead use 'return' to return from the function. For example:

```
int myFunction()
{
    // some code
    return 0; // or some other value
}
```

In this case, the program will terminate when the function returns.
